  <h1>How to Get GLP-1 Medications for Under $300/Month</h1>
  <div class="article-meta"><span>Updated Feb 2026</span><span>·</span><span>12 min read</span></div>

  <p>The biggest barrier to GLP-1 medications has never been the science — it's the price. With list prices exceeding $1,000 per month for drugs like Ozempic, Wegovy, Mounjaro, and Zepbound, millions of people who could benefit from these medications simply couldn't afford them.</p>

  <p>That's changed dramatically in 2026. Between new government pricing agreements, manufacturer savings programmes, the launch of TrumpRx, Medicare expansion, generic options, and telehealth platforms, there are now multiple legitimate ways to bring your monthly cost well below $300 — and in some cases under $50.</p>

  <p>Here's every option available right now, ranked from cheapest to most expensive.</p>

  <h2>1. Manufacturer savings cards (as low as $25/month)</h2>

  <p>If you have commercial health insurance that covers your GLP-1 medication, manufacturer savings cards are by far the cheapest option. Every major GLP-1 manufacturer offers one.</p>

  <h3>Ozempic Savings Card</h3>
  <p>Commercially insured patients with coverage can pay as little as $25 per month, with maximum savings of $100 per monthly fill. The card is valid for up to 48 months. Self-pay patients can access Ozempic through NovoCare Pharmacy at $349/month for 0.25mg, 0.5mg, or 1mg doses, and $499/month for the 2mg dose. There's also an introductory offer of $199/month for the two lowest doses for new patients filling before 31 March 2026.</p>

  <h3>Wegovy Savings Offer</h3>
  <p>Insured patients with coverage pay as little as $25/month (maximum savings $100/month). Self-pay patients can access Wegovy injections at $349/month, or the new Wegovy pill at $149/month for the 1.5mg and 4mg tablets. The 4mg tablet price increases to $199/month after 15 April 2026. Higher oral doses (9mg and 25mg) are $299/month.</p>

  <h3>Mounjaro Savings Card</h3>
  <p>Insured patients with coverage for Mounjaro for type 2 diabetes can pay as little as $25/month. If your commercial insurance doesn't cover Mounjaro, the savings card can still reduce the price by up to $150 per monthly fill.</p>

  <h3>Zepbound Savings Card</h3>
  <p>Insured patients with Zepbound coverage pay as little as $25/month. Without insurance coverage, the card can save up to $620 per monthly fill, potentially bringing the cost to around $499/month. Self-pay pricing through LillyDirect starts at $299 for the 2.5mg dose, with maintenance doses up to $449/month.</p>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>How to get a savings card:</strong> Visit the manufacturer's website (ozempic.com, wegovy.com, mounjaro.lilly.com, or zepbound.lilly.com) and look for the "Savings" or "Coverage" section. You'll fill out a short form and can download or print the card immediately. Show it at any participating pharmacy.
  </div>

  <h2>2. TrumpRx (from $149/month)</h2>

  <p>Launched in February 2026, TrumpRx.gov is a government-backed direct-to-consumer platform that connects patients to manufacturer discounts negotiated under Most-Favoured-Nation pricing agreements with Eli Lilly and Novo Nordisk.</p>

  <p>The platform is designed primarily for people paying cash — either uninsured or with insurance that doesn't cover GLP-1 medications. Here's what's available:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Oral Wegovy pill (starting doses):</strong> $149/month — the cheapest brand-name GLP-1 option available</li>
    <li><strong>Ozempic and Wegovy injections:</strong> $199–$350/month depending on dose</li>
    <li><strong>Zepbound:</strong> Starting at $299/month for the lowest dose, up to $449 for maintenance doses</li>
    <li><strong>Orforglipron</strong> (if approved): Expected starting dose of $149/month</li>
  </ul>

  <p>TrumpRx itself doesn't sell medications directly. Instead, it connects you to manufacturer discount programmes and pharmacy partners. You search for your medication on TrumpRx.gov, then download a coupon to use at participating pharmacies including CVS, Walgreens, and Costco.</p>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Important:</strong> TrumpRx coupons are for self-pay patients and cannot be combined with commercial insurance at the pharmacy counter. If your insurance already covers the medication, the manufacturer savings card (option 1 above) will almost certainly be cheaper.
  </div>

  <h2>3. Medicare coverage ($50/month copay)</h2>

  <p>For the first time in 2026, Medicare now covers GLP-1 medications for weight loss through a pilot programme. Previously, Medicare was legally prohibited from covering drugs prescribed solely for obesity.</p>

  <p>Under the new agreements, Medicare beneficiaries pay a flat $50 monthly copay for covered GLP-1 medications, including Wegovy, Ozempic, Mounjaro, and Zepbound. The government's cost has been negotiated down to $245/month per patient.</p>

  <p>To qualify, you'll need a diagnosis of obesity (BMI of 30+) or overweight (BMI of 27+) with at least one weight-related comorbidity such as type 2 diabetes, hypertension, or cardiovascular disease. Your doctor will need to confirm medical necessity.</p>

  <p>State Medicaid programmes also have access to the same $245 price point, though coverage varies by state — currently 13 states cover GLP-1 medications for obesity through Medicaid.</p>

  <h2>4. Generic liraglutide (~$200–400/month)</h2>

  <p>In August 2025, the FDA approved the first generic version of liraglutide (the active ingredient in Saxenda and Victoza), manufactured by Teva. This makes it the first generic GLP-1 available for weight loss.</p>

  <p>Generic liraglutide is significantly cheaper than brand-name weekly injectables, though it does require daily injections rather than weekly. Weight loss with liraglutide is more modest than semaglutide or tirzepatide — around 8% of body weight versus 15–22% — but for many patients it's a more affordable starting point.</p>

  <p>Additional generic liraglutide manufacturers may receive approval from late February 2026 onwards, which should drive prices down further through competition. A generic version of dulaglutide (Trulicity) could be available by 2027.</p>

  <h2>5. Telehealth platforms ($199–500/month)</h2>

  <p>Telehealth providers offer a streamlined path to GLP-1 medications — often with lower prices than traditional pharmacy channels. Most offer an online consultation, prescribing, and home delivery in a single package.</p>

  <p>Major platforms now have direct partnerships with Novo Nordisk to supply brand-name Wegovy. The current landscape includes Hims &amp; Hers, Ro, and LifeMD offering FDA-approved Wegovy through their platforms, as well as various other providers offering compounded or brand-name options.</p>

  <p>Pricing varies significantly by platform and medication type. Brand-name medications through telehealth typically cost $349–499/month (similar to manufacturer self-pay pricing), but the convenience of bundled consultations and home delivery can make this route worthwhile — especially if you don't have a local doctor experienced with GLP-1 prescribing.</p>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>A note on compounded medications:</strong> Some telehealth platforms offer compounded GLP-1 medications at lower prices ($150–300/month). These are not FDA-approved products and are produced by compounding pharmacies. The FDA has been cracking down on compounded GLP-1 products as brand-name supply shortages have resolved. If you're considering compounded options, understand the trade-offs: they're cheaper but carry more uncertainty around quality, dosing accuracy, and continued availability.
  </div>

  <h2>6. Patient assistance programmes (free)</h2>

  <p>Both Novo Nordisk and Eli Lilly offer patient assistance programmes (PAPs) that provide medication at no cost to qualifying patients. These are typically available to people who have no insurance and meet income requirements — generally a household income at or below 400% of the federal poverty level.</p>

  <p>For Novo Nordisk products (Ozempic, Wegovy), contact NovoCare at 1-866-310-7549 or visit novocare.com. For Eli Lilly products (Mounjaro, Zepbound), visit lillycares.com or call 1-800-545-6962.</p>

  <p>The application process typically involves providing proof of income and a prescription from your doctor. If approved, medication is shipped directly to your doctor's office or home.</p>

